      "latency_ms": 2225.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 521,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two atoms of **hydrogen** (H)\n*   One atom of **oxygen** (O)\n\nThese atoms are bound together by covalent bonds. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a great solvent—it can dissolve salt, sugar, and many other substances easily.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** (Rivers, oceans, rain)\n*   **Solid:** (Ice, snow, glaciers)\n*   **Gas:** (Water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\nAt standard temperature and pressure, liquid water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless. Interestingly, water defies normal physical laws when it freezes: unlike most substances, which shrink and become denser when they turn solid, **water expands and becomes less dense when it freezes**. That is why ice floats on water.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It is constantly moving through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection), cycling between the atmosphere, land, and oceans. \n\n### 4. The Biological View\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. \n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n*   Cells—the building blocks of life—are mostly filled with water, which provides the medium where metabolic and chemical reactions take place.\n*   No known organism can survive without water; it regulates body temperature, transports nutrients, and flushes out waste.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is also the lifeblood of our planet. Without it, life, weather, and the landscapes of Earth could not exist."
